1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,210
THEME SONG: In a not too
 distant future, next Sunday, AD,

2
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:12,520
there was a guy named Joel, not
 too different from you or me.

3
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:16,940
He worked at Gizmonic
 Institute, just another face

4
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:18,640
in a red jump suit.

5
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:22,480
He did a good job
 cleaning up the place,

6
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:25,420
but his bosses didn't like him,
 so they shot him into space.

7
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:33,790
We'll send him cheesy movies,
 the worst we can find.

8
00:00:33,790 --> 00:00:35,250
La, la, la.

9
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:37,670
He'll have to sit
 and watch them all,

10
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:40,680
and we'll monitor his mind.

11
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:43,520
Now, keep in mind,
 Joel can't control

12
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:47,580
where the movies begin
 or end, because he

13
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:52,370
used those special parts
 to make his robot friends.

14
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:56,890
Robot roll call.

15
00:00:56,890 --> 00:00:57,390
Cambot.

16
00:00:57,390 --> 00:00:58,370
Pan Left.

17
00:00:58,370 --> 00:00:58,870
Gypsy.

18
00:00:58,870 --> 00:00:59,670
Hi, girl.

19
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:00,450
Tom Servo.

20
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:01,900
What a cool guy.

21
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:02,400
Crow!

22
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:03,370
He's a wisecracker.

23
00:01:03,370 --> 00:01:05,320
If you're wondering how
 he eats and breathes

24
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:10,010
and other science
 facts-- la, la, la-- then

25
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:12,460
repeat to yourself,
 it's just a show.

26
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:14,920
I should really just relax.

27
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:30,630
For Mystery Science
 Theater 3000.

28
00:01:30,630 --> 00:01:31,640
-No.

29
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:34,130
No, to me, you guys, it's more
 like a moment of pure epiphany,

30
00:01:34,130 --> 00:01:36,060
you know?

31
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:36,720
Outside.

32
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:37,220
Oh, hi.

33
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:39,230
Welcome to the
 Satellite of Love.

34
00:01:39,230 --> 00:01:41,060
We'll be right with you, OK?

35
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:42,760
-Rave on, Joel Robinson.

36
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:44,260
-Rave on, my holy fool.

37
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:46,220
-All right, dig this.

38
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:47,030
[ROBOTS SCATTING]

39
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:50,340
-Once there were big
 billows of pure spirit.

40
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:52,870
I-- we'd run through
 it like a sprinkler.

41
00:01:52,870 --> 00:01:56,280
I'd try to send it
 down baffles and tubes.

42
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:59,860
Through accident and
 experiment, I worked the smoke,

43
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:04,430
keeping it at my bedside like
 a humidifier, a steady stream.

44
00:02:04,430 --> 00:02:06,530
Some nights, like a
 dream, I'd see it sputter.

45
00:02:06,530 --> 00:02:09,180
The thing is just a curl now.

46
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:13,570
Time to get on my shoes and
 find that billow of pure spirit.

47
00:02:13,570 --> 00:02:16,620
I traded away my smoke
 tools months ago.

48
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:20,460
Who needs them when you've
 got that steady stream?

49
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:22,320
VOICE (ON LOUDSPEAKER):
 Commercial sign in 15 seconds.

50
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:24,780
-All right, we're
 almost done here.

51
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:27,360
The little stream by
 my bed is at its end.

52
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:30,630
I may not even recognize
 the big bale in the cold.

53
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:33,700
But what good's life
 without some pure spirit.

54
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:35,000
VOICE (ON LOUDSPEAKER):
 Commercial sign

55
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,070
in five, four, three, two.

56
00:02:38,070 --> 00:02:39,430
Commercial sign off.

57
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:39,980
-Yes, yes.

58
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:41,210
Psycho-cali-funka-delic-expiala-dexy!

59
00:02:51,250 --> 00:02:54,770
-No, I'm pretty sure, you guys,
 I-- it's not supposed to rhyme.

60
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:55,580
It doesn't have to.

61
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:56,080
-Oh.

62
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:57,460
-Oh, kind of like a haiku.

63
00:02:57,460 --> 00:02:58,080
Mm-hmm.

64
00:02:58,080 --> 00:02:58,580
Go ahead.

65
00:02:58,580 --> 00:02:59,820
You want to do your thing now?

66
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:01,550
-Yeah.

67
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:03,550
My name is Crow.

68
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:04,540
I'm a Pisces.

69
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:06,770
And I like a girl who's quiet.

70
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:08,160
Float, floata.

71
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:11,210
-I want to-- uh,
 do you mind if I--

72
00:03:11,210 --> 00:03:11,710
-Oh.

73
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:12,460
Yeah, that's fine.

74
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:13,370
That's-- I'm done.

75
00:03:16,330 --> 00:03:19,050
-Well, hello, Sir
 Goofus Von Drake-snot.

76
00:03:19,050 --> 00:03:21,060
How's the poetry enclave going?

77
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:23,050
Still trying to teach those
 little bots about emotions,

78
00:03:23,050 --> 00:03:23,710
huh?

79
00:03:23,710 --> 00:03:25,110
Well, you caught
 us at a bad time.

80
00:03:25,110 --> 00:03:26,780
I'm right in the middle
 of finishing the elevator,

81
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:29,900
and Frank's getting
 ready for his hat party.

82
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:31,990
-Mine's going to be
 the grandest of all.

83
00:03:31,990 --> 00:03:32,690
-Yep.

84
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:35,540
Hey, it there's one thing I've
 told you about those bots,

85
00:03:35,540 --> 00:03:37,450
only hate and snap
 judgment are going

86
00:03:37,450 --> 00:03:38,770
to guide them
 through the future.

87
00:03:38,770 --> 00:03:41,360
-Uh, Dr. Forrester, you want
 to come over here a second

88
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:43,940
and-- oh, what's this for?

89
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:44,440
-No!

90
00:03:49,790 --> 00:03:50,290
-Oh.

91
00:03:50,290 --> 00:03:51,360
-Oh, man.

92
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:52,740
They crushed you, didn't they?

93
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:55,330
It's probably all hot
 and it hurts and stuff.

94
00:03:55,330 --> 00:03:58,330
Does this mean we get to do
 the invention exchange first?

95
00:03:58,330 --> 00:04:02,430
-No, you don't get to do the
 invention exchange first.

96
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:05,350
Frank, go and get that
 unicycle with the monster truck

97
00:04:05,350 --> 00:04:05,980
tire on it.

98
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:07,520
-Oh, you mean the one
 used for crushing mimes,

99
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:08,090
your naughtiness?

100
00:04:08,090 --> 00:04:10,580
-Yes, the one used
 for crushing mimes.

101
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:11,570
Frank, Frank!

102
00:04:11,570 --> 00:04:13,130
Don't let me fall.

103
00:04:13,130 --> 00:04:14,430
You make me fall,
 I'm going to shove

104
00:04:14,430 --> 00:04:17,120
bees in your nose and
 bugs down your shirt.

105
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:20,970
-Would it help to
 say, I'm sorry?

106
00:04:20,970 --> 00:04:24,120
-Frank, look at me.

107
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:26,850
OK, I'm the invention
 exchange this week.

108
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:27,640
Are you happy now?

109
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:30,690
All right, everybody gather
 around and take a big long gawk

110
00:04:30,690 --> 00:04:32,170
at the pocket scientist.

111
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:34,250
[LAUGHING]

112
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:34,900
-Wow.

113
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:35,720
[INAUDIBLE], man.

114
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:36,250
Oh.

115
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:38,970
Hey, nice improvisation
 on a standard theme.

116
00:04:38,970 --> 00:04:39,740
Joel?

117
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:40,550
-Thanks, Carl.

118
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:42,880
Well, this week's
 invention exchange for me

119
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:45,210
today is of really
 an organic nature.

120
00:04:45,210 --> 00:04:48,090
I kind of created it
 accidentally, really.

121
00:04:48,090 --> 00:04:51,260
But when you've been marooned
 in space as long as I have,

122
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:53,940
some of your personal effects
 can get to be a bit randy.

123
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:58,920
So I came up with this, the
 incredibly stinky sweat socks.

124
00:04:58,920 --> 00:04:59,830
[GROANING]

125
00:04:59,830 --> 00:05:00,870
-Works every time.

126
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:06,440
-Oh, you got it.

127
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:07,290
That's it.

128
00:05:07,290 --> 00:05:08,860
All right, OK.

129
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:09,570
Put me down.

130
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:11,050
Get off of me.

131
00:05:11,050 --> 00:05:14,490
Jerry, Jerry, lose
 the apron, all right?

132
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:16,640
And as for you, you
 barnacle built for two,

133
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:19,150
you're somehow responsible
 for my spine getting snapped.

134
00:05:19,150 --> 00:05:21,080
I'm all right, Frank.

135
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:24,580
And so you're going to
 have to pay in pain.

136
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:26,260
All right, Frank.

137
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:30,190
And your first film, rather
 fittingly, is a safety film.

138
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:31,610
And then we're going
 to follow that up

139
00:05:31,610 --> 00:05:33,960
with another
 Lippert masterpiece.

140
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:34,770
OK, Frank.

141
00:05:34,770 --> 00:05:36,460
I'm fine, all right?

142
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:38,370
And you, mow it, you taco-head.

143
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:39,200
Come on, get out of here.

144
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:39,700
Come on.

145
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:42,270
Hey, hey, hey, hey!

146
00:05:42,270 --> 00:05:43,530
[ALARM]

147
00:05:43,530 --> 00:05:44,880
-Oh, we got movie sign.

148
00:05:58,470 --> 00:06:00,400
-Hey, you don't have those
 stinky socks with you?

149
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:02,530
-Now I know who
 the stinky guy is.

150
00:06:02,530 --> 00:06:04,320
-Hey.

151
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:05,390
-Ha, "Marks the Spot."

152
00:06:05,390 --> 00:06:07,050
Is that, like, "Max, the Knife?"

153
00:06:07,050 --> 00:06:12,390
-No, I think it's about a
 dog who changed his name.

154
00:06:12,390 --> 00:06:14,800
-Look, down there,
 Raymond Carver.

155
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:16,710
This must be a short.

156
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:19,930
-And Webber the grill magnate.

157
00:06:19,930 --> 00:06:22,200
-Little bit of a
 stretch there, I'd say.

158
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:24,030
-Me?

159
00:06:24,030 --> 00:06:24,570
-Yeah.

160
00:06:24,570 --> 00:06:26,820
-And George Matthews
 as The Beaver.

161
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:28,350
-You do that every movie.

162
00:06:28,350 --> 00:06:29,960
-It's-- well, you know.

163
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:31,280
-Yeah, it's neat.

164
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:32,780
-It's in the contract.

165
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:33,450
-It's credits.

166
00:06:33,450 --> 00:06:34,210
You gotta do that.

167
00:06:34,210 --> 00:06:35,460
-And who's the commissioner?

168
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:36,990
-Arthur W. Magee.

169
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:38,510
-Arthur Magee and Molly.

170
00:06:38,510 --> 00:06:39,010
-He-he-he.

171
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:44,380
--and these fabled
 traffic accidents--

172
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:45,320
-Mm-hmm.

173
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:48,680
--involving key workers
 in New Jersey war plants--

174
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:50,130
-When did you start
 having these feelings

175
00:06:50,130 --> 00:06:51,130
that you were a commissioner?

176
00:06:51,130 --> 00:06:54,830
--makes it imperative that
 we open a second front.

177
00:06:54,830 --> 00:06:56,110
-God, I love you.

178
00:06:56,110 --> 00:06:58,640
I want to shout it
 from my oxygen tent.

179
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:00,250
-Is this for immediately
 release, Commissioner?

180
00:07:00,250 --> 00:07:03,070
-No, sit on it for a while.

181
00:07:03,070 --> 00:07:06,340
-The loss of life or
 any disabling injury

182
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:11,110
to a war worker means a definite
 setback to our war program.

183
00:07:11,110 --> 00:07:15,100
-If you kill yourselves here,
 we can't kill them over there.

184
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:15,990
-We kill, we maimed--

185
00:07:15,990 --> 00:07:17,020
-Sounds like Commissioner Fudd.

186
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:20,260
--our fellow countrymen
 without malice,

187
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:23,330
without hatred,
 without thinking.

188
00:07:23,330 --> 00:07:24,450
-Without energy.

189
00:07:24,450 --> 00:07:25,050
Would you wake up?

190
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:26,730
Come on.

191
00:07:26,730 --> 00:07:28,770
--to conserve the
 lives of the people.

192
00:07:28,770 --> 00:07:29,580
-Mm-hmm.

193
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:31,470
-And to improve
 traffic conditions.

194
00:07:31,470 --> 00:07:32,890
-Im-pwove twaffic conditions.

195
00:07:32,890 --> 00:07:34,620
-Imagine, if you
 will, a New Jersey

196
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:36,740
city with a population
 of over 20,000.

197
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:38,080
-I can almost see it.

198
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:40,210
COMMISSIONER (OFFSCREEN): Enough
 people to fill a large stadium.

199
00:07:40,210 --> 00:07:41,250
[CHEERING]

200
00:07:41,250 --> 00:07:41,750
-Free bird!

201
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:47,830
COMMISSIONER (OFFSCREEN): --are
 to suddenly removed by death

202
00:07:47,830 --> 00:07:48,570
or injury--

203
00:07:48,570 --> 00:07:50,720
-Wow, looks like a
 Gino Vannelli concert.

204
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:51,400
-Who?

205
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:54,070
--some idea of the
 annual traffic toll

206
00:07:54,070 --> 00:07:55,570
in the state of
 New Jersey alone.

207
00:07:55,570 --> 00:07:56,070
-Wow.

208
00:07:56,070 --> 00:07:58,900
You can't see it from
 here, but my torso

209
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:00,870
was fused to a block of granite.

210
00:08:00,870 --> 00:08:04,840
-But most of the drivers and
 pedestrians who were involved

211
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:06,190
didn't break all the rules.

212
00:08:06,190 --> 00:08:06,760
-Just some.

213
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:09,990
-Just once in a while, they
 were careless or inconsiderate--

214
00:08:09,990 --> 00:08:12,170
-Or they didn't want to
 live in New Jersey anymore.

215
00:08:12,170 --> 00:08:13,760
-Which is-- oh?

216
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:16,500
NARRATOR: Yes, driving and
 walking in modern traffic

217
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:18,840
is often careless,
 inconsiderate,

218
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:20,140
and from the automobile
 point of view--

219
00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:21,940
-But I get a kick out of it.

220
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:23,660
NARRATOR: --sometimes fantastic.

221
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:24,820
-It's fabulous!

222
00:08:24,820 --> 00:08:27,650
NARRATOR: --traffic is
 a bit fantastic too.

223
00:08:27,650 --> 00:08:28,570
It's about a car.

224
00:08:28,570 --> 00:08:29,070
-Bing!

225
00:08:29,070 --> 00:08:30,670
-It's called parallel
 universe parking.

226
00:08:30,670 --> 00:08:32,810
NARRATOR: --the kind you need
 these days to get home in.

227
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:35,510
And it must be a safe car.

228
00:08:35,510 --> 00:08:36,100
One that--

229
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:37,500
-This one checks out.

230
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:40,180
NARRATOR: And then there's a
 driver, a guy named Joe Doakes.

231
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:41,370
-Hi, how you doing.

232
00:08:41,370 --> 00:08:42,460
I'm Joe.

233
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:42,960
See ya.

234
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:44,710
NARRATOR: Yeah, he
 does look familiar.

235
00:08:44,710 --> 00:08:46,790
Sure, he lives on your street.

236
00:08:46,790 --> 00:08:50,300
-In fact, he's under
 your bed right now.

237
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:50,800
-Oh!

238
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:51,700
-Oh!

239
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:52,920
NARRATOR: Sorry.

240
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:54,440
Don't get sore, Joe.

241
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:57,800
Just absentminded--
 left his car in gear.

242
00:08:57,800 --> 00:08:58,750
-Let it go.

243
00:08:58,750 --> 00:08:59,980
-Oh!

244
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:01,080
NARRATOR: Well,
 what do you know?

245
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:02,880
-He uses the Braille system.

246
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:03,750
NARRATOR: That a boy, Joe.

247
00:09:03,750 --> 00:09:04,510
Count 10.

248
00:09:04,510 --> 00:09:06,780
Maybe Joe will be
 different this time.

249
00:09:06,780 --> 00:09:07,710
-Oh!

250
00:09:07,710 --> 00:09:08,690
-Oh, this is scary.

251
00:09:08,690 --> 00:09:10,660
NARRATOR: There he goes again,
 pulling away from the curb--

252
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:11,160
-Thank you.

253
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:12,400
Have a nice day.

254
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:13,530
NARRATOR: Well,
 as we were saying,

255
00:09:13,530 --> 00:09:16,610
Joe didn't have much
 patience in traffic.

256
00:09:16,610 --> 00:09:17,580
Joe used to say--

257
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:19,390
-If you're going to bury
 a body in a shallow grave,

258
00:09:19,390 --> 00:09:20,820
make sure you use Quickline.

259
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:21,760
Heh, heh!

260
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:22,870
Well, he used to say that.

261
00:09:22,870 --> 00:09:24,420
NARRATOR: Now take a
 situation like this.

262
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:26,670
If Joe were in a
 hurry, it seemed

263
00:09:26,670 --> 00:09:31,470
there was always somebody in
 his way, some inconsiderate guy.

264
00:09:31,470 --> 00:09:31,970
-Yep.

265
00:09:31,970 --> 00:09:33,740
NARRATOR: Well, if he can
 get around him and beat

266
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:34,740
that light at the next corner.

267
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:35,630
-Hey, buddy.
 What are you doing?

268
00:09:35,630 --> 00:09:36,130
-Hot dog!

269
00:09:36,130 --> 00:09:38,570
I got a date with death.

270
00:09:38,570 --> 00:09:40,440
NARRATOR: There goes the light.

271
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:41,250
-Whoa!

272
00:09:41,250 --> 00:09:42,740
NARRATOR: You see how it is?

273
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:44,900
As Joe often said--

274
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:47,400
-Oh, I've enjoyed this
 good-natured ribbing.

275
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:48,220
-Thank you!

276
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:49,190
Thank you very much.

277
00:09:49,190 --> 00:09:50,400
Thanks for almost killing me.

278
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:51,340
-Yeah, you--

279
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:51,840
-That's it.

280
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:52,590
Take me to 3rd Street.

281
00:09:52,590 --> 00:09:53,490
NARRATOR: Ah, look at that.

282
00:09:53,490 --> 00:09:54,760
As Joe says,
 traffic these days--

283
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:55,260
-Thank you.

284
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:57,560
NARRATOR: --would
 drive a guy nuts.

285
00:09:57,560 --> 00:09:58,400
[GROANING]

286
00:09:58,400 --> 00:09:59,430
NARRATOR: How do you like that?

287
00:09:59,430 --> 00:10:00,830
Didn't even close the door.

288
00:10:00,830 --> 00:10:02,230
[HONKING]

289
00:10:02,230 --> 00:10:04,300
-Hey, it's crazy Guggenheim!

290
00:10:04,300 --> 00:10:06,630
NARRATOR: Well, let's leave
 Joe to straighten this one out

291
00:10:06,630 --> 00:10:08,410
while we get on with the story.

292
00:10:08,410 --> 00:10:10,330
Now, there was a street
 intersection not far

293
00:10:10,330 --> 00:10:11,180
from where Joe lived.

294
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:11,990
-We call it Blood Alley.

295
00:10:11,990 --> 00:10:13,340
NARRATOR: You pass it every day.

296
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:16,850
No signals or stop signs, not
 much traffic, one of those

297
00:10:16,850 --> 00:10:19,080
places where nobody
 bothered to slow down--

298
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:20,410
a good place for an accident.

299
00:10:20,410 --> 00:10:21,610
-Makes you feel happy.

300
00:10:21,610 --> 00:10:23,570
NARRATOR: Here comes a car
 now from one direction.

301
00:10:23,570 --> 00:10:24,300
-What?

302
00:10:24,300 --> 00:10:25,060
NARRATOR: Uh-oh.

303
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:26,410
Here comes Joe from the other.

304
00:10:26,410 --> 00:10:28,330
-Let's watch the fun.

305
00:10:28,330 --> 00:10:30,760
We think the joke
 will be on Joe!

306
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:34,030
[TIRES SQUEALING]

307
00:10:34,030 --> 00:10:35,560
-I guess he can't be a witness.

308
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:39,900
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

309
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:40,940
--tragic accident--

310
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:42,880
--there's a sliver over there--

311
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:45,040
-(IRISH ACCENT)
 All right, folks,

312
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:46,400
there's a two drink minimum now.

313
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:48,200
NARRATOR: He's Joe Doakes,
 a neighbor of mine.

314
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:50,200
He's got a lovely wife and
 two beautiful children.

315
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:51,120
-Now I make my move.

316
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:52,550
NARRATOR: Seemed like
 a swell guy, Joe did.

317
00:10:52,550 --> 00:10:56,080
-Well, except when he ate.

318
00:10:56,080 --> 00:10:59,120
-Shows over-- or maybe not.

319
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:00,910
-Joe?

320
00:11:00,910 --> 00:11:02,450
Crypto-Joe.

321
00:11:02,450 --> 00:11:04,010
-It wasn't my fault.

322
00:11:04,010 --> 00:11:05,670
I had the right of way.

323
00:11:05,670 --> 00:11:08,430
-Ah, tell it to the corpse, pal.

324
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:10,930
-I was only doing
 15 miles an hour.

325
00:11:10,930 --> 00:11:12,710
Hey, where is that guy?

326
00:11:12,710 --> 00:11:14,340
-Uh, he's with your wife, Joe.

327
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:16,450
-I've been a careful
 driver all my life.

328
00:11:16,450 --> 00:11:17,390
-Yeah.

329
00:11:17,390 --> 00:11:18,050
-Ask my wife.

330
00:11:18,050 --> 00:11:19,570
She'll tell you.

331
00:11:19,570 --> 00:11:22,140
I've been driving for
 15 years, and I never--

332
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:23,230
-Hey.

333
00:11:23,230 --> 00:11:24,850
Is that Patrick Swayze's father?

334
00:11:24,850 --> 00:11:25,980
-Mm-hmm.

335
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:27,380
-Are you George Bailey?

336
00:11:27,380 --> 00:11:27,880
Oh, sorry.

337
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:28,510
Wrong film.

338
00:11:28,510 --> 00:11:30,070
-No use wasting time
 with these mortals.

339
00:11:30,070 --> 00:11:31,000
They can't help you anymore.

340
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,890
-Hey, it's Elton John in hell.

341
00:11:32,890 --> 00:11:34,020
--my neighbors, my friends.

342
00:11:34,020 --> 00:11:34,650
They'll understand.

343
00:11:34,650 --> 00:11:35,420
I know them.

344
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:36,650
-Yeah, you used to know them.

345
00:11:36,650 --> 00:11:37,940
-Yeah, they're your
 pallbearers now.

346
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:39,600
-Come on.

347
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:40,150
-Take my hand.

348
00:11:40,150 --> 00:11:41,870
-Upsy-daisy.

349
00:11:41,870 --> 00:11:44,410
You've got some tall
 explaining to do.

350
00:11:44,410 --> 00:11:46,340
-Do you think they're going
 to talk about how Ugly

351
00:11:46,340 --> 00:11:47,200
that guy is?

352
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:48,500
-Hm.

353
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:50,780
-Come on, let's go
 talk to Hamlet's dad.

354
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:52,420
-There's Mr. Jordan.

355
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:54,020
Hey, now over there,
 that's Mick Jagger's cloud.

356
00:11:54,020 --> 00:11:54,940
Stay off of it.

357
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:57,290
-Now, this isn't one of those
 high school dreams where

358
00:11:57,290 --> 00:11:59,780
they're going to have him
 walk around naked, or--

359
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:01,250
-I don't think so.

360
00:12:01,250 --> 00:12:04,300
-Got a class he forgot
 about until now.

361
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:05,220
Huh.

362
00:12:05,220 --> 00:12:06,650
What is this, an ice
 rink all of a sudden?

363
00:12:06,650 --> 00:12:07,150
Whoa.

364
00:12:10,070 --> 00:12:11,370
-I'll be with you in a moment.

365
00:12:11,370 --> 00:12:13,990
I'm just sealing some fates.

366
00:12:13,990 --> 00:12:17,580
-Today, in dead people's court.

367
00:12:17,580 --> 00:12:20,870
-Uh-- ahem-- uh, excuse me?

368
00:12:20,870 --> 00:12:22,680
Uh, pardon your--

369
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:23,330
-Mr. God?

370
00:12:23,330 --> 00:12:24,110
-Sir?

371
00:12:24,110 --> 00:12:26,130
-Dear diary, it's
 hard being God.

372
00:12:26,130 --> 00:12:26,630
Huh?

373
00:12:26,630 --> 00:12:30,450
Oh, later.

374
00:12:30,450 --> 00:12:34,260
-Oh, where are
 your skates, boys?

375
00:12:34,260 --> 00:12:35,030
-Oh, my line.

376
00:12:35,030 --> 00:12:36,280
Sorry-- and my hat.

377
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:38,370
Excuse me, your hon-- your sir.

378
00:12:38,370 --> 00:12:40,950
-Good morning, sir.

379
00:12:40,950 --> 00:12:43,010
-There are no good
 mornings here.

380
00:12:43,010 --> 00:12:44,460
-I'll decide if it's
 a good morning or not.

381
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:47,150
-What was your name?

382
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:48,270
-What was my name?

383
00:12:48,270 --> 00:12:48,770
-Huh?

384
00:12:48,770 --> 00:12:50,590
-You mean, sir, I'm finished?

385
00:12:50,590 --> 00:12:51,400
All washed up?

386
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:51,990
-86'ed?

387
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:53,690
-Well, that depends
 on the evidence.

388
00:12:53,690 --> 00:12:54,830
-Hasta luego, Buckwheat?

389
00:12:54,830 --> 00:12:55,400
-86 holiday?

390
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:57,410
-Your name?

391
00:12:57,410 --> 00:12:59,370
JOE (OFFSCREEN):
 Joseph Doakes, sir.

392
00:12:59,370 --> 00:13:00,520
-Joseph Doakes.

393
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:01,090
-Funny name, huh.

394
00:13:03,590 --> 00:13:04,870
-Doakes--

395
00:13:04,870 --> 00:13:05,530
-Doakes, let's see.

396
00:13:05,530 --> 00:13:08,770
There's dickweed, dog,
 dumb sh-- ah, here it is.

397
00:13:08,770 --> 00:13:10,070
-Joe Doakes.

398
00:13:10,070 --> 00:13:10,990
-Hm.

399
00:13:10,990 --> 00:13:13,230
-Ah, you were born
 in New Jersey, eh?

400
00:13:13,230 --> 00:13:14,790
-I thought I smelled something.

401
00:13:14,790 --> 00:13:16,020
-Your age?

402
00:13:16,020 --> 00:13:17,770
-I'm going to be 36
 on my next birthday.

403
00:13:17,770 --> 00:13:18,270
[CHUCKLING]

404
00:13:18,270 --> 00:13:19,560
-Yeah, right.

405
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:21,330
-Next birthday?

406
00:13:21,330 --> 00:13:24,280
-Boy, God sure has a
 crummy office, doesn't he?

407
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:25,140
-Why, he wasn't due here--

408
00:13:25,140 --> 00:13:26,710
-That's because all the
 decorators are in hell.

409
00:13:26,710 --> 00:13:27,630
--for another 20 years.

410
00:13:27,630 --> 00:13:29,900
Fine guardian you
 turned out to be.

411
00:13:29,900 --> 00:13:31,230
-Why, if it wasn't
 for me, your honor,

412
00:13:31,230 --> 00:13:32,520
he'd have been here years ago.

413
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:34,420
The worst driver in
 New Jersey, and you

414
00:13:34,420 --> 00:13:35,650
expect me to keep
 him out of trouble.

415
00:13:35,650 --> 00:13:38,550
-My records show no
 convictions against this man--

416
00:13:38,550 --> 00:13:39,690
-Oh, he might go free.

417
00:13:39,690 --> 00:13:42,380
-Yeah, he did a favor
 for Sinatra once.

418
00:13:42,380 --> 00:13:43,740
-I've been driving
 for 15 years--

419
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:46,250
-Oh, silence.

420
00:13:46,250 --> 00:13:48,350
This court isn't
 interested in your opinion.

421
00:13:48,350 --> 00:13:50,160
-Oh!

422
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:51,060
-Proceed.

423
00:13:51,060 --> 00:13:53,740
-Don't you see, your honor, that
 record of no convictions only

424
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:55,620
proves how well I've guided him.

425
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:57,840
-You see, I stopped a
 car with my face once.

426
00:13:57,840 --> 00:14:00,430
My forehead's all [INAUDIBLE].

427
00:14:00,430 --> 00:14:02,200
-Well, he wasn't a bad fella.

428
00:14:02,200 --> 00:14:03,170
-No.

429
00:14:03,170 --> 00:14:04,020
-Good natured egg.

430
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:04,640
-Egg?

431
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:05,310
-Kind to his family.

432
00:14:05,310 --> 00:14:05,830
-Hard boiled.

433
00:14:05,830 --> 00:14:07,540
-Very considerate of everyone.

434
00:14:07,540 --> 00:14:08,160
-Poached?

435
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:08,660
Often.

436
00:14:08,660 --> 00:14:09,620
-You see, judge?

437
00:14:09,620 --> 00:14:11,290
I'm not such a bad guy.

438
00:14:11,290 --> 00:14:14,170
Please let me go
 back, just this once?

439
00:14:14,170 --> 00:14:15,370
Besides--

440
00:14:15,370 --> 00:14:17,520
-Hey, he's got a gun?

441
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:20,220
-I got almost a full book
 of eight coupons left.

442
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:21,940
-Well, you should have
 presented those immediately

443
00:14:21,940 --> 00:14:24,120
upon ordering.

444
00:14:24,120 --> 00:14:25,760
-Let's get on with the charges.

445
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:26,680
-Visa or Mastercard?

446
00:14:26,680 --> 00:14:27,230
Ha, ha!

447
00:14:27,230 --> 00:14:27,930
Little--

448
00:14:27,930 --> 00:14:29,680
-How is he on remembering
 to give signals?

449
00:14:32,850 --> 00:14:34,250
-[INAUDIBLE] the body
 of the letter, huh?

450
00:14:34,250 --> 00:14:35,210
-On signals?

451
00:14:35,210 --> 00:14:36,500
Well, only fair.

452
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:38,800
Quite a few black
 marks against him.

453
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:41,890
But when it came to sounding
 his horn, he was pretty bad.

454
00:14:41,890 --> 00:14:43,690
He was always sneaking
 up and scaring

455
00:14:43,690 --> 00:14:45,110
the wits out of pedestrians.

456
00:14:45,110 --> 00:14:45,890
-Don't get me wrong.

457
00:14:45,890 --> 00:14:46,660
I enjoyed that.

458
00:14:46,660 --> 00:14:47,820
--about night driving?

459
00:14:47,820 --> 00:14:49,270
-Hm.

460
00:14:49,270 --> 00:14:49,950
-I brought a clip.

461
00:14:49,950 --> 00:14:51,080
I don't think it
 needs any set up.

462
00:14:51,080 --> 00:14:52,650
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 --called for more caution

463
00:14:52,650 --> 00:14:53,840
in driving in the daytime.

464
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:55,970
But I couldn't get to
 first base with him.

465
00:14:55,970 --> 00:14:57,450
-It was our first date.

466
00:14:57,450 --> 00:14:58,230
What a night.

467
00:14:58,230 --> 00:14:59,330
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 --the glare

468
00:14:59,330 --> 00:15:01,360
of his headlights
 did to other drivers.

469
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:02,350
Hm.

470
00:15:02,350 --> 00:15:04,630
Well, there's a lot of little
 things here, your honor, but--

471
00:15:04,630 --> 00:15:06,700
-Well, then what the hell
 are you wasting my time for?

472
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:08,740
--show much consideration
 for the other guy.

473
00:15:08,740 --> 00:15:12,140
-Hm, a perfect description
 of the average driver.

474
00:15:12,140 --> 00:15:15,150
-Joe was all right until
 he got out on the highway.

475
00:15:15,150 --> 00:15:17,180
Then he was an entirely
 different person.

476
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:20,190
-He became alarmed by my
 modeled face and bumpy skull.

477
00:15:20,190 --> 00:15:22,600
--careless,
 impatient, irritable.

478
00:15:22,600 --> 00:15:24,980
I can't say that Joe was out
 looking for trouble, but--

479
00:15:24,980 --> 00:15:26,650
-But I did find him
 down by the waterfront,

480
00:15:26,650 --> 00:15:27,640
dressed in a Spartan costume.

481
00:15:27,640 --> 00:15:29,940
-Kitty, kitty.

482
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:31,500
-Don.

483
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:34,310
-You know, I'm thinking
 of growing a goatee.

484
00:15:34,310 --> 00:15:38,580
-That description fitted
 someone else I know.

485
00:15:38,580 --> 00:15:40,100
If you know who I mean.

486
00:15:40,100 --> 00:15:40,890
-Hitler?

487
00:15:40,890 --> 00:15:41,520
Uh, no.

488
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:42,180
He drove a stick.

489
00:15:42,180 --> 00:15:43,020
-Yes, your honor.

490
00:15:43,020 --> 00:15:45,550
I had to learn the hard way too.

491
00:15:45,550 --> 00:15:46,380
-Shut up.

492
00:15:55,050 --> 00:15:57,610
-The toughest job I had
 with Joe was his speeding.

493
00:15:57,610 --> 00:16:00,030
-Yeah, he was worse than
 Corey Feldman or Corey Haim.

494
00:16:00,030 --> 00:16:01,600
-Even if he did keep
 within legal speed limits,

495
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:03,580
he might still be
 going too fast for--

496
00:16:03,580 --> 00:16:04,940
-Oh!

497
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:05,490
Whoa--

498
00:16:05,490 --> 00:16:07,310
-Rockford turn.

499
00:16:07,310 --> 00:16:08,620
Backwards Starsky--

500
00:16:08,620 --> 00:16:09,920
-Oh, that was great!

501
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:11,990
-Momentum was just another
 name in the physics book,

502
00:16:11,990 --> 00:16:13,690
as far as Joe was concerned.

503
00:16:13,690 --> 00:16:14,890
-Hey, that was a low blow.

504
00:16:14,890 --> 00:16:16,090
-Judge, if I weren't
 a ghost, there

505
00:16:16,090 --> 00:16:19,530
were times when I would
 have been scared to death.

506
00:16:19,530 --> 00:16:22,620
Joe's specialty was a right turn
 from the center of the street.

507
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:24,780
He used to wow
 them with that one.

508
00:16:24,780 --> 00:16:25,910
-He was great.

509
00:16:25,910 --> 00:16:26,930
What an artist!

510
00:16:26,930 --> 00:16:28,030
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 --concerned

511
00:16:28,030 --> 00:16:29,450
about the drivers he'd cut off.
 -Wow.

512
00:16:29,450 --> 00:16:30,280
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 Or the pedestrians

513
00:16:30,280 --> 00:16:31,380
who had the right
 of way at the--

514
00:16:31,380 --> 00:16:32,350
--Whoa!

515
00:16:32,350 --> 00:16:33,180
-Wow.

516
00:16:33,180 --> 00:16:36,180
-And then when we'd
 approach a stop sign,

517
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:37,750
he'd never stop dead.

518
00:16:37,750 --> 00:16:38,580
Oh, no.

519
00:16:38,580 --> 00:16:40,330
He'd come down to a slow roll.

520
00:16:40,330 --> 00:16:41,200
-Hey, he feels bad enough.

521
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:42,830
-Sneak through, and
 then give it the gas.

522
00:16:42,830 --> 00:16:44,640
-And then give them the bird.

523
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:45,740
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 However, he

524
00:16:45,740 --> 00:16:47,890
did learn one thing
 that seemed to stick.

525
00:16:47,890 --> 00:16:48,390
-Gum.

526
00:16:48,390 --> 00:16:49,560
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 One day, when

527
00:16:49,560 --> 00:16:51,270
he was driving in a
 residential section,

528
00:16:51,270 --> 00:16:55,460
approaching the playground of
 a school, without any warning,

529
00:16:55,460 --> 00:16:57,950
a ball bounced out, directly
 in front of the car.

530
00:16:57,950 --> 00:16:58,570
-I'm huge.

531
00:16:58,570 --> 00:17:00,430
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN): And
 right behind it came a kid.

532
00:17:00,430 --> 00:17:03,550
The little fella disappeared
 behind the hood of a car.

533
00:17:03,550 --> 00:17:04,060
-What?

534
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:05,720
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 Joe jammed on the brakes,

535
00:17:05,720 --> 00:17:07,980
started to jump out of the car.

536
00:17:07,980 --> 00:17:10,140
Then the little boy ran out with
 the ball clutched in his hands.

537
00:17:10,140 --> 00:17:11,430
-With his skull
 clutched in his hands.

538
00:17:11,430 --> 00:17:13,130
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 --unaware of the close call.

539
00:17:13,130 --> 00:17:15,010
Joe slumped back into
 the driver's seat.

540
00:17:15,010 --> 00:17:17,690
-He said a silent prayer, to
 Bongo, the god of gravity.

541
00:17:17,690 --> 00:17:19,450
-After that, I never
 had any trouble with Joe

542
00:17:19,450 --> 00:17:20,400
around schools.

543
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:22,740
He'd crawl by at a snail's pace.

544
00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:24,220
-Nursing homes, though.

545
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:24,720
Whoa!

546
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:27,560
-You see, Joe had two kids
 of his own at that school.

547
00:17:27,560 --> 00:17:29,090
-And if his wife
 ever found out--

548
00:17:29,090 --> 00:17:31,920
-They're all that way when
 it gets close to home.

549
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:34,160
A pity they don't
 always drive as

550
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:36,960
though their own
 families were in danger.

551
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:39,800
-You make me sick.

552
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:42,210
-There was another
 bad habit Joe had.

553
00:17:42,210 --> 00:17:43,650
[CRASHING]

554
00:17:46,910 --> 00:17:48,180
-Oh wait, you'll
 want to hear this.

555
00:17:48,180 --> 00:17:50,910
This is good.

556
00:17:50,910 --> 00:17:52,650
-I love Fibber Magee and Molly.

557
00:17:55,880 --> 00:17:58,100
-And at the halftime,
 well, injuries

558
00:17:58,100 --> 00:18:00,710
are ahead of fatalities
 here in New Jersey.

559
00:18:05,370 --> 00:18:07,670
-I'm so ashamed.

560
00:18:07,670 --> 00:18:10,580
-Gulp.

561
00:18:10,580 --> 00:18:13,000
-Uh-huh.

562
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,070
-Wow, looks like, uh,
 Nixon's enemy list.

563
00:18:16,070 --> 00:18:17,150
-Scratch another one.

564
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:21,820
-Hurts, doesn't it?

565
00:18:21,820 --> 00:18:23,890
-So, go on with
 your little story.

566
00:18:23,890 --> 00:18:25,320
Go ahead, puny man.

567
00:18:28,280 --> 00:18:29,950
-As I was saying,
 sir, one of Joe's

568
00:18:29,950 --> 00:18:32,500
worst habits with hogging
 the center of the road.

569
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:35,090
If some driver behind
 Joe honked his horn,

570
00:18:35,090 --> 00:18:38,690
well, Joe would lose his temper
 and start to put on speed.

571
00:18:38,690 --> 00:18:41,480
But if Joe was behind
 some other driver, well,

572
00:18:41,480 --> 00:18:42,650
that was different.

573
00:18:42,650 --> 00:18:44,450
He'd cut out with
 a burst of speed,

574
00:18:44,450 --> 00:18:47,080
not knowing what was
 behind or in front of him.

575
00:18:47,080 --> 00:18:50,090
Then one day, we were
 behind another car,

576
00:18:50,090 --> 00:18:51,400
climbing a steep hill.

577
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:52,110
-See?

578
00:18:52,110 --> 00:18:53,540
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 Joe became impatient.

579
00:18:53,540 --> 00:18:56,050
And then came the truck.

580
00:18:56,050 --> 00:18:57,970
Joe saw the danger.

581
00:18:57,970 --> 00:19:01,480
Oh, he was always quick to
 see the danger to himself.

582
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:02,550
The truck was bearing down.

583
00:19:02,550 --> 00:19:03,740
-Uh-- whoa!

584
00:19:03,740 --> 00:19:05,040
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 Joe cut backside.

585
00:19:05,040 --> 00:19:05,590
-Whoa.

586
00:19:05,590 --> 00:19:06,680
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 He made it.

587
00:19:06,680 --> 00:19:08,230
But the poor guy behind him--

588
00:19:08,230 --> 00:19:09,680
-The poor guy was James Dean.

589
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:10,870
The end.

590
00:19:10,870 --> 00:19:13,150
-Sir, even a ghost has feelings.

591
00:19:13,150 --> 00:19:15,700
Why, I was so disgusted
 with his selfishness--

592
00:19:15,700 --> 00:19:18,170
-Why, I booed him,
 right in his front seat.

593
00:19:18,170 --> 00:19:20,310
-If I hadn't given
 you my solemn promise

594
00:19:20,310 --> 00:19:22,250
to stick it out with
 him, I would have.

595
00:19:22,250 --> 00:19:23,490
-Just relax, now.

596
00:19:23,490 --> 00:19:24,250
Take it easy.

597
00:19:24,250 --> 00:19:27,930
-Sorry I blew up like
 that, your-- um--

598
00:19:27,930 --> 00:19:29,030
-By the way--

599
00:19:29,030 --> 00:19:30,130
-What are you
 doing Friday night?

600
00:19:30,130 --> 00:19:33,680
--does he ever drive
 when he's been drinking?

601
00:19:33,680 --> 00:19:34,210
-Oh, boy.

602
00:19:34,210 --> 00:19:36,190
I need my other scroll for that.

603
00:19:36,190 --> 00:19:39,330
That's a doozy.

604
00:19:39,330 --> 00:19:40,750
-Well, sir, he--

605
00:19:40,750 --> 00:19:44,460
-Guardian, I want
 the whole truth.

606
00:19:44,460 --> 00:19:46,050
-So help me me.

607
00:19:46,050 --> 00:19:49,200
-Well, sir, Joe did
 take a nip occasionally.

608
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:51,860
He wasn't really what
 you'd call a drinker.

609
00:19:51,860 --> 00:19:53,390
Just a cocktail now and then.

610
00:19:53,390 --> 00:19:54,570
-Just a half gallon
 now and then.

611
00:19:54,570 --> 00:19:56,660
-You know how the
 boys are, your honor.

612
00:19:56,660 --> 00:19:58,460
-Yes, I know how the boys are.

613
00:19:58,460 --> 00:19:59,840
-And I know where the boys are.

614
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:03,150
-I have a book full of those
 "just a cocktail or two"

615
00:20:03,150 --> 00:20:03,650
drinkers.

616
00:20:03,650 --> 00:20:05,160
-Jerks wouldn't let
 me in their club.

617
00:20:05,160 --> 00:20:08,020
-Drivers and pedestrians too.

618
00:20:08,020 --> 00:20:09,740
Dead ones.

619
00:20:09,740 --> 00:20:10,630
-Big ones.

620
00:20:10,630 --> 00:20:12,620
-Just a cocktail or two.

621
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:13,500
-Phooey!

622
00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:17,680
-Any abnormal physical or
 mental condition due to drinking

623
00:20:17,680 --> 00:20:18,270
liquor--

624
00:20:18,270 --> 00:20:18,960
-Is monotonous.

625
00:20:18,960 --> 00:20:21,620
--is intoxication in this state.

626
00:20:21,620 --> 00:20:25,200
The careless driver or
 pedestrian is usually careless

627
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:28,190
regarding the other
 fellow's rights and safety.

628
00:20:28,190 --> 00:20:30,920
But as to his own
 safety and well being--

629
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:32,050
-Mm-hmm.

630
00:20:32,050 --> 00:20:33,990
--he's downright selfish.

631
00:20:33,990 --> 00:20:35,300
-Pah!

632
00:20:35,300 --> 00:20:39,770
-Now, dilute that
 dangerous frame of mind

633
00:20:39,770 --> 00:20:41,970
with just a cocktail or two--

634
00:20:41,970 --> 00:20:42,470
-Oh, OK.

635
00:20:42,470 --> 00:20:42,970
Thanks.

636
00:20:42,970 --> 00:20:43,740
I'll take scotch.

637
00:20:43,740 --> 00:20:47,160
--then he even forgets
 about his own safety.

638
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:48,790
That is one of the
 reasons why that clock--

639
00:20:48,790 --> 00:20:50,300
-Is this going to
 be on the final?

640
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:52,300
--ticked off a traffic
 accident in this state--

641
00:20:52,300 --> 00:20:55,060
-Hey, if we're talking mob
 murders, we'd be digital.

642
00:20:55,060 --> 00:20:56,130
[HUMMING A FIGHT SONG]

643
00:20:59,350 --> 00:21:02,970
-Incidentally, was there any
 traffic rule he didn't violate?

644
00:21:02,970 --> 00:21:03,510
-Yes, sir.

645
00:21:03,510 --> 00:21:04,890
I never hit and run.

646
00:21:04,890 --> 00:21:06,810
-Oh, well, that
 changes everything.

647
00:21:06,810 --> 00:21:08,610
-If you had, you would
 have been booked in--

648
00:21:08,610 --> 00:21:09,300
-Ahem.

649
00:21:09,300 --> 00:21:10,350
-In phlegm?

650
00:21:10,350 --> 00:21:13,000
-In a lower court.

651
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:16,160
So you never hit and run.

652
00:21:16,160 --> 00:21:18,480
Why, that's so
 despicable an act--

653
00:21:18,480 --> 00:21:19,080
-Des-peck-a-pick?

654
00:21:19,080 --> 00:21:21,830
--that I wouldn't even
 accuse you of doing it.

655
00:21:21,830 --> 00:21:23,360
-Never hit and run?

656
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:24,380
Why, I ought to throw the--

657
00:21:24,380 --> 00:21:26,720
-Was his car
 regularly inspected?

658
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:27,640
-Yes, sir.

659
00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:29,210
-Hey, I'm no Commie.

660
00:21:29,210 --> 00:21:30,450
-Oh, yeah.

661
00:21:30,450 --> 00:21:31,980
At the last minute.

662
00:21:31,980 --> 00:21:34,760
But you'll never know how I
 had to nag at his conscience

663
00:21:34,760 --> 00:21:36,910
to get him over
 for an inspection.

664
00:21:36,910 --> 00:21:39,800
Why, I had to throw all the
 statistics in the book at him.

665
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:43,840
Over 256,000 cars rejected
 for punked steering gears.

666
00:21:43,840 --> 00:21:44,870
-Oh, Joe.

667
00:21:44,870 --> 00:21:49,020
-And over 800,000 cars
 rejected for dangerous brakes.

668
00:21:49,020 --> 00:21:51,470
And over 1,400,000--

669
00:21:51,470 --> 00:21:53,600
-OK, thank you, Mr. Spock.

670
00:21:53,600 --> 00:21:55,500
-And those figures
 always got him.

671
00:21:55,500 --> 00:21:56,080
-Uh-huh.

672
00:21:56,080 --> 00:21:57,450
-Why, here's proof
 that I got him

673
00:21:57,450 --> 00:21:59,680
to keep his car in
 good running condition.

674
00:21:59,680 --> 00:22:02,150
He can't blame his
 accident on his car.

675
00:22:02,150 --> 00:22:04,010
-Well, we'll mark
 that exhibit B.

676
00:22:04,010 --> 00:22:05,920
-And I'll slit my wrist
 with it, because I'm

677
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:07,130
bored by this whole thing.

678
00:22:07,130 --> 00:22:10,450
-He is exhibit A. I've
 heard about enough of him

679
00:22:10,450 --> 00:22:12,140
as a driver.

680
00:22:12,140 --> 00:22:14,550
-Now, how is he as
 a cocktail waitress?

681
00:22:14,550 --> 00:22:17,400
-Well, sir, as a pedestrian,
 he was different.

682
00:22:17,400 --> 00:22:20,330
As a driver, he thought
 he owned the highway,

683
00:22:20,330 --> 00:22:22,390
but as a pedestrian,
 he knew he owned it.

684
00:22:22,390 --> 00:22:23,140
-Oh.

685
00:22:23,140 --> 00:22:25,170
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN): He'd weave
 through traffic like a mouse

686
00:22:25,170 --> 00:22:26,100
through a maze--

687
00:22:26,100 --> 00:22:27,720
-Squeaking and calling
 himself Algernon.

688
00:22:27,720 --> 00:22:29,550
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
 --behind a wheel.

689
00:22:29,550 --> 00:22:30,990
And heaven help the
 driver who might

690
00:22:30,990 --> 00:22:33,320
come close enough
 to frighten Joe.

691
00:22:33,320 --> 00:22:34,620
-And bless the beasts
 and the children.

692
00:22:34,620 --> 00:22:35,200
Oh.

693
00:22:35,200 --> 00:22:36,810
-One day we were at
 a busy intersection.

694
00:22:36,810 --> 00:22:39,740
My mind was on other
 things for a minute.

695
00:22:39,740 --> 00:22:42,810
As the red light flashed
 on, Joe got away from me

696
00:22:42,810 --> 00:22:44,950
and started diagonally
 across the intersection.

697
00:22:44,950 --> 00:22:46,680
-Doh.

698
00:22:46,680 --> 00:22:49,430
-Now, instead of walking
 over to this corner

699
00:22:49,430 --> 00:22:54,090
and then over to this corner,
 Joe decides to take a shortcut.

700
00:22:54,090 --> 00:22:57,090
He had, uh, just about
 reached this point--

701
00:22:57,090 --> 00:22:59,130
-That's when he
 stepped into a wormhole

702
00:22:59,130 --> 00:23:01,040
and was transported
 to the 16th century.

703
00:23:04,710 --> 00:23:06,250
-I see.

704
00:23:06,250 --> 00:23:08,540
You tried to get
 here earlier, eh?

705
00:23:08,540 --> 00:23:09,730
-Yes, sir.

706
00:23:09,730 --> 00:23:11,390
He almost made it too.

707
00:23:11,390 --> 00:23:14,540
He found out the hypotenuse
 of a triangle in traffic

708
00:23:14,540 --> 00:23:16,570
is the shortest road
 to the hospital.

709
00:23:16,570 --> 00:23:17,880
-Oh, that was a good one.

710
00:23:17,880 --> 00:23:19,520
You looked like a
 hamburger that time.

711
00:23:19,520 --> 00:23:21,110
-Two long, happy months.

712
00:23:21,110 --> 00:23:23,430
My only rest in
 the 15 long years

713
00:23:23,430 --> 00:23:25,420
that I had to spend with
 that guy's conscience.

714
00:23:25,420 --> 00:23:26,780
-Aw.

715
00:23:26,780 --> 00:23:28,560
-(SIGHING) Ah, what happy days.

716
00:23:28,560 --> 00:23:31,790
There I was with not
 a care in the world.

717
00:23:31,790 --> 00:23:32,590
-Get on with it.

718
00:23:32,590 --> 00:23:34,970
-Just as long as he kept out
 of traffic, it was wonderful.

719
00:23:34,970 --> 00:23:37,370
But of course, all good
 things come to an end.

720
00:23:37,370 --> 00:23:38,890
-Including the Wildwood weed.

721
00:23:41,510 --> 00:23:43,110
-Well, for the
 next few weeks, Joe

722
00:23:43,110 --> 00:23:45,570
was the perfect
 pedestrian and driver.

723
00:23:45,570 --> 00:23:49,300
Then one night, he-- he, uh,
 stayed up with a sick friend.

724
00:23:49,300 --> 00:23:50,750
-What's that?

725
00:23:50,750 --> 00:23:51,310
-Well, uh--

726
00:23:51,310 --> 00:23:52,590
-Well, he got ploughed, OK?

727
00:23:52,590 --> 00:23:53,640
--he had a cocktail or two.

728
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:54,140
-Jiminy.

729
00:23:54,140 --> 00:23:57,150
-But maybe it was like he said,
 it was the hamburgers he ate.

730
00:23:57,150 --> 00:23:59,830
Anyways, the next morning,
 his head throbbed so hard,

731
00:23:59,830 --> 00:24:00,770
I almost felt it.

732
00:24:00,770 --> 00:24:01,870
-He was higher
 than Judy Garland.

733
00:24:01,870 --> 00:24:04,930
-And in that condition, he got
 behind the wheel of his car.

734
00:24:04,930 --> 00:24:07,290
And that had to be his
 last day, your honor.

735
00:24:07,290 --> 00:24:08,820
Ooh, what a pity.

736
00:24:08,820 --> 00:24:10,510
He really went to
 town or somewhere.

737
00:24:10,510 --> 00:24:13,680
-He landed right on the
 X. That takes direction.

738
00:24:13,680 --> 00:24:16,060
-From sheer exhaustion,
 I closed my eyes

739
00:24:16,060 --> 00:24:19,330
for just a fraction of a second.

740
00:24:19,330 --> 00:24:20,540
I failed you, sir.

741
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:21,900
-Who's on trial here?

742
00:24:21,900 --> 00:24:25,080
-I was so weary, nothing
 seemed to matter.

743
00:24:25,080 --> 00:24:27,300
-Oh.

744
00:24:27,300 --> 00:24:28,470
-15 years--

745
00:24:28,470 --> 00:24:29,710
-On a dead man's chest.

746
00:24:29,710 --> 00:24:31,860
--as Joe Doakes'
 driving conscience.

747
00:24:31,860 --> 00:24:32,470
-Sorry.

748
00:24:32,470 --> 00:24:36,790
-Is Punishment enough,
 even for a ghost.

749
00:24:36,790 --> 00:24:38,670
You have completely
 paid the penalty

750
00:24:38,670 --> 00:24:42,040
for your own
 carelessness in traffic.

751
00:24:42,040 --> 00:24:43,620
You are now a free ghost.

752
00:24:43,620 --> 00:24:45,700
-See the clerk,
 and leave the gown.

753
00:24:45,700 --> 00:24:46,550
-Go?

754
00:24:46,550 --> 00:24:47,050
I can go?

755
00:24:47,050 --> 00:24:48,110
-Free ghost!

756
00:24:48,110 --> 00:24:49,050
-Oh!

757
00:24:49,050 --> 00:24:50,010
-"Stairway to Heaven."

758
00:24:50,010 --> 00:24:50,970
-Woo!

759
00:24:50,970 --> 00:24:51,860
-What a relief.

760
00:24:51,860 --> 00:24:52,710
What a relief.

761
00:24:52,710 --> 00:24:54,080
-The hell with Joe.

762
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:54,690
I'm out of here.

763
00:24:57,640 --> 00:25:01,190
-He was a-- with the--

764
00:25:01,190 --> 00:25:02,090
-It's high noon.

765
00:25:05,480 --> 00:25:06,940
[CRASHING]

766
00:25:06,940 --> 00:25:08,680
-Oh, that's the angel leaving.

767
00:25:08,680 --> 00:25:09,590
He's dead now.

768
00:25:09,590 --> 00:25:12,930
-Oh, I think that's James Dean
 or Isadora Duncan or maybe

769
00:25:12,930 --> 00:25:14,920
Jayne Mansfield.

770
00:25:14,920 --> 00:25:15,860
-Hm.

771
00:25:15,860 --> 00:25:17,260
[HUMMING A FIGHT SONG]

772
00:25:21,440 --> 00:25:23,420
-Can I watch some
 TV now, grandpa?

773
00:25:26,010 --> 00:25:26,520
-Stop it.

774
00:25:26,520 --> 00:25:27,320
Stop it.
 -What?

775
00:25:27,320 --> 00:25:28,020
What am I doing?

776
00:25:28,020 --> 00:25:28,730
What?

777
00:25:28,730 --> 00:25:32,120
-Stop it?

778
00:25:32,120 --> 00:25:35,030
Oh, I wish I could stop it.

779
00:25:35,030 --> 00:25:36,760
-Oh, I wish I were dead.

780
00:25:36,760 --> 00:25:40,070
Oh, I am.

781
00:25:40,070 --> 00:25:43,810
-Joseph Doakes, as to
 passing sentence upon you,

782
00:25:43,810 --> 00:25:48,730
I must leave that to this jury
 of drivers and pedestrians.

783
00:25:48,730 --> 00:25:49,340
-Oh, guilty.

784
00:25:49,340 --> 00:25:49,840
Hang him.

785
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:50,770
String him up.

786
00:25:50,770 --> 00:25:52,400
And get the director too.

787
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:54,360
-Ladies and gentlemen
 of the jury--

788
00:25:54,360 --> 00:25:54,860
-Jury?

789
00:25:54,860 --> 00:25:55,430
-Huh?

790
00:25:55,430 --> 00:25:57,560
-Before you retire
 to pass judgement--

791
00:25:57,560 --> 00:25:58,290
-Oh, that's great.

792
00:25:58,290 --> 00:26:02,110
We're stranded in space, and we
 still have to pull jury duty.

793
00:26:02,110 --> 00:26:03,300
-Not me.

794
00:26:03,300 --> 00:26:04,280
-Hey, this is important.

795
00:26:04,280 --> 00:26:05,230
Pay attention.

796
00:26:05,230 --> 00:26:07,340
-He was kind and
 considerate to everyone.

797
00:26:07,340 --> 00:26:09,640
-Except his grandmother
 and the kitty.

798
00:26:09,640 --> 00:26:10,260
-OK, all right.

799
00:26:10,260 --> 00:26:11,290
Come on.
 -Let's get out of here, come on.

800
00:26:11,290 --> 00:26:11,930
-Come on.

801
00:26:11,930 --> 00:26:12,980
-We're blowing out of here.

802
00:26:12,980 --> 00:26:14,040
You can just hang around.

803
00:26:14,040 --> 00:26:15,190
You can stay, Crow.

804
00:26:15,190 --> 00:26:19,440
-He was inconsiderate on
 dimming his headlights.

805
00:26:19,440 --> 00:26:22,160
He wasn't too
 careful about speed.

806
00:26:22,160 --> 00:26:22,660
-Mm-mm.

807
00:26:22,660 --> 00:26:23,490
Mm-mm.

808
00:26:23,490 --> 00:26:26,460
-He seldom came to a full stop.

809
00:26:26,460 --> 00:26:27,140
-I remember that.

810
00:26:27,140 --> 00:26:28,040
-At stop signs.

811
00:26:28,040 --> 00:26:29,360
-Yep.

812
00:26:29,360 --> 00:26:31,990
-He frequently hogged the road--

813
00:26:31,990 --> 00:26:32,640
-Hogging the road.
 Right.

814
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:33,140
I remember.

815
00:26:33,140 --> 00:26:37,280
--and sometimes passed on hills.

816
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:40,480
I must say, in
 his favor, that he

817
00:26:40,480 --> 00:26:42,800
did have regular
 car inspections.

818
00:26:42,800 --> 00:26:44,570
-Yeah.

819
00:26:44,570 --> 00:26:45,510
-And now--

820
00:26:45,510 --> 00:26:46,010
-Ah!

821
00:26:46,010 --> 00:26:53,420
--most importantly, before you
 hand in your verdict, think.

822
00:26:53,420 --> 00:26:57,210
Consider carefully
 your own qualifications

823
00:26:57,210 --> 00:26:59,710
as a driver and a pedestrian.

824
00:26:59,710 --> 00:27:01,390
-My qualifications?

825
00:27:01,390 --> 00:27:03,290
-Are you qualified--

826
00:27:03,290 --> 00:27:04,570
-Am I qualified?

827
00:27:04,570 --> 00:27:06,300
--to pass judgment on Joe?

828
00:27:06,300 --> 00:27:07,050
-Yeah, I don't know.

829
00:27:07,050 --> 00:27:08,190
I'm not qualified.

830
00:27:23,590 --> 00:27:26,260
-Hm, qualified.

831
00:27:26,260 --> 00:27:27,840
Qualified.

832
00:27:27,840 --> 00:27:30,260
Wow, what a question.

833
00:27:30,260 --> 00:27:31,590
Me, Crow T. Robot.

834
00:27:31,590 --> 00:27:32,380
What do I think?

835
00:27:32,380 --> 00:27:33,370
Am I qualified?

836
00:27:33,370 --> 00:27:35,300
Wow, that's a heavy burden.

837
00:27:35,300 --> 00:27:36,950
How can I make a difference?

838
00:27:36,950 --> 00:27:38,980
Can I make a difference?

839
00:27:38,980 --> 00:27:41,130
Surely I'm but a
 single bot, alone

840
00:27:41,130 --> 00:27:44,440
as it were in the
 vast universe thing.

841
00:27:44,440 --> 00:27:45,910
Maybe I can't change the world.

842
00:27:45,910 --> 00:27:49,270
Perhaps I've looked at
 life from upside down.

843
00:27:49,270 --> 00:27:50,350
Hm.

844
00:27:50,350 --> 00:27:55,140
Hey Cambot, move it in a little,
 and cue that moody music.

845
00:27:55,140 --> 00:27:58,010
Well, what would Joel do
 in a situation like this?

846
00:27:58,010 --> 00:27:58,510
No.

847
00:27:58,510 --> 00:27:59,010
No.

848
00:27:59,010 --> 00:28:03,660
No, no-- I've got to learn
 to think for myself, to stand

849
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:05,670
on my own two
 foot-like appendages.

850
00:28:05,670 --> 00:28:08,100
Seize the day, yeah.

851
00:28:08,100 --> 00:28:09,830
Think globally, act locally.

852
00:28:09,830 --> 00:28:12,800
Yes, by God, I can do it.

853
00:28:12,800 --> 00:28:16,030
Why, I could start a
 letter-writing campaign.

854
00:28:16,030 --> 00:28:17,000
Yeah, that would help.

855
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:19,410
And uh, I could
 organize a bake sale.

856
00:28:19,410 --> 00:28:23,280
Or hey, we could all help.

857
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:24,230
Come on, friends.

858
00:28:24,230 --> 00:28:26,160
Run to your window and
 shout, I'm really cheesed,

859
00:28:26,160 --> 00:28:29,670
and I'm not going to hang around
 till this thing gets better.

860
00:28:29,670 --> 00:28:32,770
Why, organize an improv group
 and do guerrilla theater

861
00:28:32,770 --> 00:28:35,010
at the food court in your mall.

862
00:28:35,010 --> 00:28:36,330
Dress a little differently.

863
00:28:36,330 --> 00:28:38,730
Make it more exciting
 for you and your spouse.

864
00:28:38,730 --> 00:28:40,910
Or here's an idea-- toss
 a little Cajun spice

865
00:28:40,910 --> 00:28:43,820
into the party mix
 and watch the fun.

866
00:28:43,820 --> 00:28:46,620
Put on a one-man show and talk
 about your true inner feelings

867
00:28:46,620 --> 00:28:48,930
in an emotionally-charged,
 gut-wrenching, autobiographical

868
00:28:48,930 --> 00:28:50,240
account of your
 warped adolescence,

869
00:28:50,240 --> 00:28:51,720
and then watch the
 grant money come in.

870
00:28:51,720 --> 00:28:52,220
Woo!

871
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:53,620
But don't snap judge me.

872
00:28:53,620 --> 00:28:56,930
And then watch that-- I
 know, put a drop of vanilla

873
00:28:56,930 --> 00:28:59,190
behind each ear, and you'll
 smell like a cookie all day.

874
00:28:59,190 --> 00:29:02,060
Or, uh, eat an apple,
 nature's toothbrush.

875
00:29:02,060 --> 00:29:03,360
Ask Mr. Owl how
 many licks it takes

876
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:05,510
to get to the Tootsie center.

877
00:29:05,510 --> 00:29:07,870
Have you met everyone
 on your block?

878
00:29:07,870 --> 00:29:10,410
Now would be a nice time
 to start, don't you think?

879
00:29:10,410 --> 00:29:11,540
Hm?

880
00:29:11,540 --> 00:29:13,670
In the classroom, slide
 your desks together

881
00:29:13,670 --> 00:29:15,330
and create an ecology symbol.

882
00:29:15,330 --> 00:29:17,800
Um, police the lives
 of those around you,

883
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:21,220
and get your sensibilities
 way the heck out of whack.

884
00:29:21,220 --> 00:29:23,450
Parade up and down the
 street in your underwear.

885
00:29:23,450 --> 00:29:24,920
Impose your ideals on others.

886
00:29:24,920 --> 00:29:25,560
It's easy.

887
00:29:25,560 --> 00:29:28,320
Crush someone with an emotional
 word or an enigmatic look.

888
00:29:28,320 --> 00:29:29,180
You decide.

889
00:29:29,180 --> 00:29:29,800
You do it.

890
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:30,730
I'm sick of this.

891
00:29:30,730 --> 00:29:31,880
I can't make a decision.

892
00:29:31,880 --> 00:29:34,060
I'm no good at
 this sort of thing.

893
00:29:34,060 --> 00:29:35,360
It's up to you.

894
00:29:35,360 --> 00:29:40,220
I'm passing the buck to you,
 and I've got commercial sign.

895
00:29:40,220 --> 00:29:44,310
Oh, the pain, the pain.

896
00:29:44,310 --> 00:29:44,810
[SIGH]

897
00:29:54,230 --> 00:29:55,930
-Oh no, Robert Lippert?

898
00:29:55,930 --> 00:29:56,790
Oh, yikes.

899
00:29:56,790 --> 00:29:58,810
-[INAUDIBLE] is
 extending us a welcome.

900
00:29:58,810 --> 00:30:01,150
-Bedside potatoes.

901
00:30:01,150 --> 00:30:03,030
-"King Dinosaur."

902
00:30:03,030 --> 00:30:04,440
-Breakfast with the king or--

903
00:30:04,440 --> 00:30:05,680
-I can't do that.

904
00:30:05,680 --> 00:30:06,600
Excuse me.

905
00:30:06,600 --> 00:30:07,220
-You OK, Crow?

906
00:30:07,220 --> 00:30:08,450
-Oh, yeah.

907
00:30:08,450 --> 00:30:09,660
Yeah.

908
00:30:09,660 --> 00:30:11,950
What-- what is this?

909
00:30:11,950 --> 00:30:12,720
-Bryant Curtis?

910
00:30:12,720 --> 00:30:14,020
-"King Dinosaur."

911
00:30:14,020 --> 00:30:14,520
-Oh.

912
00:30:14,520 --> 00:30:15,300
-It's a Lippert film.

913
00:30:15,300 --> 00:30:16,230
[SCREAMING]

914
00:30:16,230 --> 00:30:17,160
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

915
00:30:17,160 --> 00:30:18,090
We know.

916
00:30:21,820 --> 00:30:22,810
Pretty sad, huh?

917
00:30:22,810 --> 00:30:23,310
-Oh my gosh.

918
00:30:23,310 --> 00:30:24,570
-Marvin Miller?

919
00:30:24,570 --> 00:30:26,510
This must be his crossing.

920
00:30:26,510 --> 00:30:28,090
With the movie and--

921
00:30:28,090 --> 00:30:28,590
-Yeah.

922
00:30:28,590 --> 00:30:29,560
-Joel and Ethan Coen.

923
00:30:32,950 --> 00:30:36,810
There's a-- it's a
 Zimbalists in this film.

924
00:30:36,810 --> 00:30:39,160
I think there was some nepotism.

925
00:30:39,160 --> 00:30:41,250
There's Ralph Helfer.

926
00:30:41,250 --> 00:30:43,040
I hear he's a big cow.

927
00:30:43,040 --> 00:30:47,620
Again, with the
 heifer and-- sorry.

928
00:30:47,620 --> 00:30:51,700
-Ah, based on an original
 story by August Strindberg.

929
00:30:51,700 --> 00:30:54,250
"Beast From Outer
 Space," his first wife.

930
00:30:54,250 --> 00:30:56,770
-Tom Gries, and the whole
 world Gries with him.

931
00:30:56,770 --> 00:30:59,410
-That's as bad as mine.

932
00:30:59,410 --> 00:31:02,250
-It's Al Bert I.,
 and Zimbalist Gordon.

933
00:31:02,250 --> 00:31:03,120
-They're credits.

934
00:31:03,120 --> 00:31:04,460
-Oh, I'm filled with wonder.

935
00:31:04,460 --> 00:31:07,300
These titles have whisked
 me away to a magic land.

936
00:31:07,300 --> 00:31:08,300
-Oh, don't go away, titles.

937
00:31:08,300 --> 00:31:10,730
NARRATOR: On April 23, the word
 to start comes from Washington.

938
00:31:10,730 --> 00:31:11,220
-Start.

939
00:31:11,220 --> 00:31:13,290
NARRATOR: Immediately, our
 finest research laboratories

940
00:31:13,290 --> 00:31:15,360
change over to this
 all-important project,

941
00:31:15,360 --> 00:31:18,280
the building of a passenger
 carrying rocket ship.

942
00:31:18,280 --> 00:31:19,590
Every security measure is taken.

943
00:31:19,590 --> 00:31:21,560
-What you see, what you hear,
 what you leave-- Burma-Shave,

944
00:31:21,560 --> 00:31:22,060
huh.

945
00:31:22,060 --> 00:31:24,000
NARRATOR: Test upon
 test must be made

946
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:25,600
to develop, improve, create--

947
00:31:25,600 --> 00:31:30,160
-The ultimate morning
 zoo radio show.

948
00:31:30,160 --> 00:31:31,850
NARRATOR: A jet
 engine must be built

949
00:31:31,850 --> 00:31:33,340
with a thrust great
 enough to launch

950
00:31:33,340 --> 00:31:35,640
the spaceship through the many
 miles of Earth's atmosphere.

951
00:31:35,640 --> 00:31:37,670
-If you lean to the side, this
 looks like a burning building.

952
00:31:37,670 --> 00:31:39,570
NARRATOR: --withstand the
 pressures found in outer space.

953
00:31:43,820 --> 00:31:46,210
-Then the world's largest
 roll-on deodorant was invented.

954
00:31:46,210 --> 00:31:48,010
NARRATOR: --unusally favorable
 atmospheric conditions,

955
00:31:48,010 --> 00:31:50,660
Professor Albert Garnett is
 able with the world's largest

956
00:31:50,660 --> 00:31:52,850
telescope to
 photograph an extremely

957
00:31:52,850 --> 00:31:53,870
clear view of a new planet.

958
00:31:53,870 --> 00:31:55,070
-But he had his
 thumb over the lens.

959
00:31:55,070 --> 00:31:56,940
NARRATOR: It is now certain
 that plant life of some kind

960
00:31:56,940 --> 00:31:58,670
definitely exists there.

961
00:31:58,670 --> 00:31:59,870
-Tony Danza, for instance.

962
00:31:59,870 --> 00:32:00,040
NARRATOR: The
 professor, incidentally,

963
00:32:00,040 --> 00:32:02,020
is also credited with
 naming the new planet.

964
00:32:02,020 --> 00:32:03,010
-Planet Alan.

965
00:32:03,010 --> 00:32:03,740
NARRATOR: Planet Nova.

966
00:32:03,740 --> 00:32:04,430
-Oh.

967
00:32:04,430 --> 00:32:06,250
-Close.

968
00:32:06,250 --> 00:32:07,690
NARRATOR: New metals
 must be developed,

969
00:32:07,690 --> 00:32:09,860
with tensile strength
 capable of withstanding

970
00:32:09,860 --> 00:32:13,310
great atmospheric pressures.

971
00:32:13,310 --> 00:32:14,240
-Lumps.

972
00:32:14,240 --> 00:32:16,370
NARRATOR: Switch on for
 jet engine test number 87.

973
00:32:16,370 --> 00:32:20,870
-Oh, so the announcer is
 calling the shots now?

974
00:32:20,870 --> 00:32:23,210
-Ooh.

975
00:32:23,210 --> 00:32:27,390
-I think their
 dryer is off-kilter.

976
00:32:27,390 --> 00:32:31,660
NARRATOR: Structural weaknesses
 are studied, tested--

977
00:32:31,660 --> 00:32:34,580
-Oh, here the effects of 13
 rum and Diet Cokes are tested.

978
00:32:34,580 --> 00:32:37,110
NARRATOR: Every conceivable
 test for man and machine alike

979
00:32:37,110 --> 00:32:38,070
has run the gauntlet.

980
00:32:38,070 --> 00:32:39,630
There is no margin for error.

981
00:32:39,630 --> 00:32:41,330
-There is a margin
 for shame, however.

982
00:32:41,330 --> 00:32:42,710
NARRATOR: Special
 equipment is developed.

983
00:32:42,710 --> 00:32:43,240
-Then discarded.

984
00:32:43,240 --> 00:32:44,380
NARRATOR: This
 nuclear power plant

985
00:32:44,380 --> 00:32:46,590
will serve as an auxiliary
 source of electricity

986
00:32:46,590 --> 00:32:48,740
while our people
 are on the planet.

987
00:32:48,740 --> 00:32:51,190
Actually, because it is
 activated by atomic power--

988
00:32:51,190 --> 00:32:52,680
-Oh, he was just
 kidding earlier.

989
00:32:52,680 --> 00:32:54,560
NARRATOR: --for many years
 if something were to go wrong

990
00:32:54,560 --> 00:32:55,860
and their return to
 earth were delayed.

991
00:32:55,860 --> 00:32:57,430
-Come on, it looks
 like a dashboard.

992
00:32:57,430 --> 00:32:58,380
Oh, port mole.

993
00:32:58,380 --> 00:32:59,580
NARRATOR: Naturally,
 the people must

994
00:32:59,580 --> 00:33:00,900
be careful in using
 such equipment,

995
00:33:00,900 --> 00:33:04,190
because if the atomic power
 were allowed to go unharnessed,

996
00:33:04,190 --> 00:33:06,730
an atomic fission
 reaction would take place.

997
00:33:06,730 --> 00:33:07,390
-Ooh.

998
00:33:07,390 --> 00:33:08,480
-Ooh.

999
00:33:08,480 --> 00:33:10,850
-Better one?

1000
00:33:10,850 --> 00:33:12,050
Better four?

1001
00:33:12,050 --> 00:33:13,890
-I better reset the watch here.

1002
00:33:13,890 --> 00:33:15,440
-Ah, way off.

1003
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:17,110
NARRATOR: They, of course,
 will understand its use

1004
00:33:17,110 --> 00:33:19,060
and therefore
 minimize any danger.

1005
00:33:19,060 --> 00:33:22,790
-The Brady house,
 built tall and strong.

1006
00:33:22,790 --> 00:33:24,670
NARRATOR: Pressure
 vaults are constructed

1007
00:33:24,670 --> 00:33:27,490
to simulate the
 pressures found in space.

1008
00:33:27,490 --> 00:33:30,200
-And the pressures of my
 desolate lonely life, sitting

1009
00:33:30,200 --> 00:33:33,210
for hours alone
 in a sound booth.

1010
00:33:33,210 --> 00:33:35,620
NARRATOR: Record
 every vibrating pulse.

1011
00:33:35,620 --> 00:33:36,880
-Oh, yes!

1012
00:33:36,880 --> 00:33:37,790
-Oh.

1013
00:33:37,790 --> 00:33:38,290
Yes.

1014
00:33:38,290 --> 00:33:40,310
NARRATOR: Turbo jet
 engines used in assisting

1015
00:33:40,310 --> 00:33:42,610
takeoffs or our giant
 bombers are studied.

1016
00:33:42,610 --> 00:33:44,680
But there lies the
 greatest problem.

1017
00:33:44,680 --> 00:33:45,520
Blasting the ship--

1018
00:33:45,520 --> 00:33:47,540
-Uh, looks like he's burning
 some oil, doesn't it?

1019
00:33:47,540 --> 00:33:50,250
-Yeah.

1020
00:33:50,250 --> 00:33:52,560
-Then German air
 shows are performed.

1021
00:33:52,560 --> 00:33:53,470
Hundreds are killed.

1022
00:33:59,240 --> 00:34:01,170
Hi!

1023
00:34:01,170 --> 00:34:02,750
NARRATOR: The first
 test rocket is ready.

1024
00:34:02,750 --> 00:34:05,210
-Switch on for launching.

1025
00:34:05,210 --> 00:34:07,070
-Hey, they flew it out
 of the Eiffel Tower.

1026
00:34:07,070 --> 00:34:08,390
NARRATOR: Rocket
 test number two.

1027
00:34:08,390 --> 00:34:11,830
-That was number two!

1028
00:34:11,830 --> 00:34:14,540
NARRATOR: This rocket
 carries animals, white mice--

1029
00:34:14,540 --> 00:34:16,720
-Hi, my name is Benji, and
 I'm a 10-dimensional being.

1030
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:18,490
NARRATOR: --gravity is still
 present to hold the mice

1031
00:34:18,490 --> 00:34:20,080
and little ball
 downward toward Earth.

1032
00:34:23,010 --> 00:34:24,870
-Whoa!

1033
00:34:24,870 --> 00:34:26,300
NARRATOR: Now the mice
 are floating in air,

1034
00:34:26,300 --> 00:34:28,710
since they are now beyond
 Earth's gravity pull.

1035
00:34:28,710 --> 00:34:30,270
-Hey, why do we have
 to do all the work?

1036
00:34:30,270 --> 00:34:31,780
I'm not feeling too good.

1037
00:34:31,780 --> 00:34:34,010
NARRATOR: The test proves out.

1038
00:34:34,010 --> 00:34:35,750
Animals can live in space.

1039
00:34:35,750 --> 00:34:36,380
It's man--

1040
00:34:36,380 --> 00:34:37,310
-What are you, man or mouse?

1041
00:34:37,310 --> 00:34:39,430
-Well, put some cheese down
 there, and you'll find out.

1042
00:34:39,430 --> 00:34:40,530
NARRATOR: The
 spaceship is ready.

1043
00:34:40,530 --> 00:34:44,000
Now for its passengers.

1044
00:34:44,000 --> 00:34:45,040
-Hey, Keith Richards.

1045
00:34:45,040 --> 00:34:47,320
NARRATOR: If animal life is
 found on the new planet Nova,

1046
00:34:47,320 --> 00:34:50,720
an expert on zoogeography would
 be a most important member

1047
00:34:50,720 --> 00:34:51,850
of the space expedition.

1048
00:34:51,850 --> 00:34:52,690
-Calcified and--

1049
00:34:52,690 --> 00:34:54,780
NARRATOR: On August
 10, Dr. Richard Gordon

1050
00:34:54,780 --> 00:34:56,650
was chosen to fill
 that position.

1051
00:34:56,650 --> 00:34:58,250
He became famous
 with his discovery

1052
00:34:58,250 --> 00:35:00,980
of the giant prehistoric tar
 pits near Salt Lake City,

1053
00:35:00,980 --> 00:35:01,720
just two years ago.

1054
00:35:01,720 --> 00:35:03,390
-I'd say this patient is dead.

1055
00:35:03,390 --> 00:35:05,630
I'm no expert, but
 dead's the word.

1056
00:35:05,630 --> 00:35:07,460
NARRATOR: The study of rock
 formations and its minerals

1057
00:35:07,460 --> 00:35:09,910
is like reading the
 personal diary of a planet.

1058
00:35:09,910 --> 00:35:11,130
-Yes, it's not very polite.

1059
00:35:11,130 --> 00:35:12,900
NARRATOR: Dr. Nora Pearce
 joined the space expedition

1060
00:35:12,900 --> 00:35:14,690
on August 27.

1061
00:35:14,690 --> 00:35:16,370
Her doctorate in
 mineralogy was awarded

1062
00:35:16,370 --> 00:35:18,870
for her mineralogical research
 in the Himalayan Mountains.

1063
00:35:18,870 --> 00:35:20,650
-And she's cute too.

1064
00:35:20,650 --> 00:35:22,280
NARRATOR: That's the day
 the giant spaceship was

1065
00:35:22,280 --> 00:35:23,080
being wheeled into the--

1066
00:35:23,080 --> 00:35:23,710
-Fire.

1067
00:35:23,710 --> 00:35:24,870
NARRATOR: --for
 its space flight,

1068
00:35:24,870 --> 00:35:26,080
which was now a
 matter of weeks--

1069
00:35:26,080 --> 00:35:27,030
-Painted up by hand.

1070
00:35:27,030 --> 00:35:28,190
-Nice color.

1071
00:35:28,190 --> 00:35:28,690
-Yeah.

1072
00:35:28,690 --> 00:35:29,190
Lovely.

1073
00:35:29,190 --> 00:35:30,330
Great fins.

1074
00:35:30,330 --> 00:35:32,610
NARRATOR: Medicine must be
 represented on this expedition.

1075
00:35:32,610 --> 00:35:33,540
-Because they'll need drugs.

1076
00:35:33,540 --> 00:35:34,680
NARRATOR: --people
 and the people

1077
00:35:34,680 --> 00:35:36,370
that will venture into
 space in the future

1078
00:35:36,370 --> 00:35:37,560
is of primary importance.

1079
00:35:37,560 --> 00:35:38,290
-OK, you may spit now.

1080
00:35:38,290 --> 00:35:40,950
NARRATOR: Dr. Ralph Martin's
 war service fortified him

1081
00:35:40,950 --> 00:35:43,090
with the experience of
 treating most diseases

1082
00:35:43,090 --> 00:35:44,560
and fatalities
 that overtake men.

1083
00:35:44,560 --> 00:35:45,580
-Hey, he's cute too, isn't he?

1084
00:35:45,580 --> 00:35:46,080
-Mm-hmm.

1085
00:35:46,080 --> 00:35:46,910
Mm-hmm.

1086
00:35:46,910 --> 00:35:47,520
-Real cute.

1087
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:49,090
NARRATOR: The chemistry
 of the new planet

1088
00:35:49,090 --> 00:35:51,530
was to be studied by Dr.
 Patricia Bennett, who

1089
00:35:51,530 --> 00:35:52,680
completed the group
 of scientists.

1090
00:35:52,680 --> 00:35:53,340
-Drink it.
 Drink it.

1091
00:35:53,340 --> 00:35:54,670
NARRATOR: She was
 noted for her thesis

1092
00:35:54,670 --> 00:35:56,850
on the use or
 radiochemistry in medicine.

1093
00:35:56,850 --> 00:35:58,350
-And, uh, she's cute too.

1094
00:35:58,350 --> 00:36:01,410
NARRATOR: --on September 12.

1095
00:36:01,410 --> 00:36:03,160
-Hm, dirty.

1096
00:36:03,160 --> 00:36:04,740
NARRATOR: On October
 1, it was observed

1097
00:36:04,740 --> 00:36:08,070
that the position of Nova was in
 the most advantageous location

1098
00:36:08,070 --> 00:36:09,120
for our purpose.

1099
00:36:09,120 --> 00:36:12,130
The ship must blast
 off within 24 hours.

1100
00:36:12,130 --> 00:36:13,810
-Or someone's going to pay.

1101
00:36:13,810 --> 00:36:14,700
Oh, brother.

1102
00:36:17,680 --> 00:36:18,180
-Uh-oh.

1103
00:36:18,180 --> 00:36:22,070
Is Kooky the Clown on
 the premises again?

1104
00:36:22,070 --> 00:36:23,330
NARRATOR: Attention--

1105
00:36:23,330 --> 00:36:24,260
-It's me, the announcer.

1106
00:36:24,260 --> 00:36:25,790
This time I'm coming
 from a different speaker.

1107
00:36:25,790 --> 00:36:29,200
NARRATOR: --the expedition
 will please report to the ship.

1108
00:36:29,200 --> 00:36:31,940
Blast off is in X
 minus 20 minutes.

1109
00:36:31,940 --> 00:36:34,170
-Will the artist please
 come to the stage, please?

1110
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:39,940
-I wonder what
 this button's for.

1111
00:36:43,350 --> 00:36:45,870
NARRATOR: Attention, blast
 off in X minus 5 minutes.

1112
00:36:45,870 --> 00:36:46,370
-Hey, look.

1113
00:36:46,370 --> 00:36:49,990
It's Woodstock-- three days of
 peace, love, and stock footage.

1114
00:36:49,990 --> 00:36:52,670
-Stay away from the towers.

1115
00:36:52,670 --> 00:36:53,350
-You look neat.

1116
00:36:57,620 --> 00:36:58,680
-10 seconds tick away.

1117
00:36:58,680 --> 00:37:01,910
The stock footage is rolled.

1118
00:37:01,910 --> 00:37:03,220
NARRATOR: Blast off in X minus--

1119
00:37:03,220 --> 00:37:04,240
-All right, all right.

1120
00:37:04,240 --> 00:37:04,740
I know.

1121
00:37:08,980 --> 00:37:10,270
-A new pope is selected.

1122
00:37:10,270 --> 00:37:11,270
It's Kooky.

1123
00:37:11,270 --> 00:37:12,720
-Neat.

1124
00:37:12,720 --> 00:37:14,520
NARRATOR: Five,
 four, three, two--

1125
00:37:14,520 --> 00:37:15,270
-Ho, ho, whoa!

1126
00:37:15,270 --> 00:37:16,210
-That's a fast count.

1127
00:37:19,480 --> 00:37:19,980
-Huh.

1128
00:37:19,980 --> 00:37:22,600
Well, maybe you should take
 your foot off the clutch.

1129
00:37:22,600 --> 00:37:25,050
There you go.

1130
00:37:25,050 --> 00:37:26,510
-It's hard to get
 it out of first.

1131
00:37:26,510 --> 00:37:27,480
-Yeah.

1132
00:37:27,480 --> 00:37:27,980
-Whoa.

1133
00:37:27,980 --> 00:37:29,450
-It's a German rocket, you know.

1134
00:37:29,450 --> 00:37:30,420
-Whoa!

1135
00:37:30,420 --> 00:37:31,890
-That's farfegnugen.

1136
00:37:31,890 --> 00:37:32,870
-Whoa!

1137
00:37:32,870 --> 00:37:34,340
Oh, no!

1138
00:37:43,230 --> 00:37:45,240
-I believe by the
 end of this decade,

1139
00:37:45,240 --> 00:37:48,120
we will land a piece of stock
 footage on another planet.

1140
00:37:48,120 --> 00:37:52,280
Ask not what a process
 shot can do for you.

1141
00:37:52,280 --> 00:37:54,690
-Very good.

1142
00:37:54,690 --> 00:37:55,240
-Uh-oh.

1143
00:37:55,240 --> 00:37:57,030
It's a handicap spot.

1144
00:37:57,030 --> 00:37:59,040
Hey, and it's not close
 enough to the mall.

1145
00:38:03,770 --> 00:38:05,840
-Guys, I think they
 landed in Wisconsin.

1146
00:38:05,840 --> 00:38:06,470
-Yeah.

1147
00:38:06,470 --> 00:38:08,840
Uh, they just discovered
 Grizzly Adams.

1148
00:38:12,450 --> 00:38:16,430
-Pff, uh, that's one
 small step for a man,

1149
00:38:16,430 --> 00:38:20,220
uh, one giant step
 into a cowpie.

1150
00:38:20,220 --> 00:38:20,720
-Ew.

1151
00:38:20,720 --> 00:38:21,210
-Over.

1152
00:38:21,210 --> 00:38:22,890
Pff.

1153
00:38:22,890 --> 00:38:24,410
-Cute, huh?

1154
00:38:24,410 --> 00:38:28,030
-They're going all this way
 just to make out in a field?

1155
00:38:28,030 --> 00:38:30,920
-Uh, well--

1156
00:38:30,920 --> 00:38:34,700
-At least their face
 shields are interlocking.

1157
00:38:34,700 --> 00:38:36,590
-Hey, Joel, is she a
 bubble-headed blonde?

1158
00:38:36,590 --> 00:38:39,500
-Uh, yeah.

1159
00:38:39,500 --> 00:38:40,540
-We're first.

1160
00:38:40,540 --> 00:38:41,610
-And foremost.

1161
00:38:41,610 --> 00:38:43,350
-Thank god we made it.

1162
00:38:43,350 --> 00:38:44,130
-Mm.

1163
00:38:44,130 --> 00:38:45,150
-Is it as lovely as it seems?

1164
00:38:45,150 --> 00:38:45,650
-Well--

1165
00:38:45,650 --> 00:38:46,200
-No.

1166
00:38:46,200 --> 00:38:46,700
-Uh, yeah.

1167
00:38:49,540 --> 00:38:50,410
-No.

1168
00:38:50,410 --> 00:38:52,090
-An active volcano.

1169
00:38:52,090 --> 00:38:53,860
This planet is quite young, Pat.

1170
00:38:53,860 --> 00:38:55,270
-I mean, uh, Dr. Pat.

1171
00:38:55,270 --> 00:38:56,950
-They're getting
 kind of informal.

1172
00:38:56,950 --> 00:38:57,450
-Yeah.

1173
00:38:57,450 --> 00:38:58,820
-Mm-hmm.

1174
00:38:58,820 --> 00:38:59,320
-Cash.

1175
00:38:59,320 --> 00:39:00,610
-We're going to
 start out test, Dick,

1176
00:39:00,610 --> 00:39:01,520
to see if it's safe
 to remove our suits.

1177
00:39:01,520 --> 00:39:02,290
-What did he call him?

1178
00:39:02,290 --> 00:39:03,620
-What did he just say?

1179
00:39:03,620 --> 00:39:06,000
-Your instruments are
 on their way down.

1180
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:07,380
Hurry up with the
 test, so Nora and I

1181
00:39:07,380 --> 00:39:08,580
can get out and join you, huh?

1182
00:39:08,580 --> 00:39:10,490
-Yeah, we want to
 frolic in the raw too.

1183
00:39:13,340 --> 00:39:16,480
-Oh, thank goodness the
 time constant is safe.

1184
00:39:16,480 --> 00:39:18,400
-Whoops.

1185
00:39:18,400 --> 00:39:20,400
-Why is he blow drying
 the grass, guys?

1186
00:39:20,400 --> 00:39:21,610
-Because he can.

1187
00:39:21,610 --> 00:39:22,110
-Oh.

1188
00:39:26,450 --> 00:39:27,330
-Hey look, honey.

1189
00:39:27,330 --> 00:39:28,630
There's change everywhere.

1190
00:39:28,630 --> 00:39:30,820
This is better
 than Coney Island.

1191
00:39:30,820 --> 00:39:35,000
-(ECHOING THE SOUNDTRACK)
 Do-do-do-do, Do-do-do.

1192
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:37,780
-Why, uh, this is smoke-able.

1193
00:39:37,780 --> 00:39:38,280
Hm.

1194
00:39:38,280 --> 00:39:41,170
High grade hydro.

1195
00:39:41,170 --> 00:39:42,620
-Chunky black.

1196
00:39:42,620 --> 00:39:45,030
Hm.

1197
00:39:45,030 --> 00:39:47,670
-Hey, I can see my brain.

1198
00:39:47,670 --> 00:39:48,840
-(ECHOING THE
 SOUNDTRACK) Do-do-do.

1199
00:39:52,340 --> 00:39:54,560
-Oh, let's see what this one
 is-- "Origin of a Planet?"

1200
00:39:54,560 --> 00:39:58,020
Oh, I've got that on CD.

1201
00:39:58,020 --> 00:40:00,750
-(ECHOING THE
 SOUNDTRACK) Do-do-do.

1202
00:40:00,750 --> 00:40:02,680
-Is Pete Fountain around here?

1203
00:40:02,680 --> 00:40:05,830
-A preliminary biological
 study made of the air on Nova--

1204
00:40:05,830 --> 00:40:06,330
-Who's talking?

1205
00:40:06,330 --> 00:40:08,700
--show the indications
 of microscopic life

1206
00:40:08,700 --> 00:40:11,200
not too different from
 that found on Earth.

1207
00:40:11,200 --> 00:40:13,540
However, approximately 40% of--

1208
00:40:13,540 --> 00:40:14,410
-Did I bring this with me?

1209
00:40:14,410 --> 00:40:15,260
--the bacteria--

1210
00:40:15,260 --> 00:40:15,860
-Was superimposed.

1211
00:40:15,860 --> 00:40:17,840
--was completely unfamiliar.

1212
00:40:17,840 --> 00:40:21,090
-Test for radiation gave a
 reading of approximately 10%

1213
00:40:21,090 --> 00:40:22,750
above normal background count.

1214
00:40:22,750 --> 00:40:23,650
-Joel, I'm tripping.

1215
00:40:23,650 --> 00:40:25,630
-No, it's just a photo process.

1216
00:40:25,630 --> 00:40:26,600
-A Coppola film.

1217
00:40:26,600 --> 00:40:28,620
-Yeah.

1218
00:40:28,620 --> 00:40:29,910
-Temperature--

1219
00:40:29,910 --> 00:40:30,840
-Expletive deleted.

1220
00:40:30,840 --> 00:40:33,110
18 minutes was deleted
 from this tape.

1221
00:40:33,110 --> 00:40:35,350
I am not a crook.

1222
00:40:35,350 --> 00:40:38,720
-Although our preliminary tests
 are in no way conclusive--

1223
00:40:38,720 --> 00:40:39,640
-Or any good at all.

1224
00:40:39,640 --> 00:40:43,510
--it does substantiate, however,
 that human and animal life

1225
00:40:43,510 --> 00:40:46,620
as we know it on Earth can
 exist in the atmosphere

1226
00:40:46,620 --> 00:40:49,190
and environment of Planet Nova.

1227
00:40:49,190 --> 00:40:50,730
-The end.

1228
00:40:50,730 --> 00:40:53,450
-Oh, they taped over the
 Beatles Berlin tapes.

1229
00:40:53,450 --> 00:40:57,210
[GROANING]

1230
00:40:57,210 --> 00:40:58,680
-Now, lets get out
 of these suits.

1231
00:40:58,680 --> 00:41:00,430
-And into a dry martini.

1232
00:41:00,430 --> 00:41:02,720
-Nora, the atmosphere
 checks out all right.

1233
00:41:02,720 --> 00:41:04,580
It's safe to come out.

1234
00:41:04,580 --> 00:41:05,250
-Hm.

1235
00:41:05,250 --> 00:41:07,970
He means, uh-- high five.

1236
00:41:07,970 --> 00:41:08,700
Oh, that's Patty-cake.

1237
00:41:08,700 --> 00:41:13,110
That's the international symbol
 for "off with your clothes."

1238
00:41:13,110 --> 00:41:14,860
-Oh, what a day.

1239
00:41:14,860 --> 00:41:17,550
Patty, I'll cover your rear.

1240
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:27,730
-The air's a bit
 richer in oxygen.

1241
00:41:27,730 --> 00:41:28,230
Notice?

1242
00:41:28,230 --> 00:41:30,200
-Yep, I'm high as a kite.

1243
00:41:30,200 --> 00:41:32,520
-Anyway, it'll speed
 up our reaction.

1244
00:41:32,520 --> 00:41:34,120
-Oh, you take care of
 your own reaction, doctor.

1245
00:41:34,120 --> 00:41:35,170
-Ho-ho.

1246
00:41:35,170 --> 00:41:36,010
-What do you think Nora?

1247
00:41:36,010 --> 00:41:37,030
-This is just great.

1248
00:41:37,030 --> 00:41:37,790
[LAUGHTER]

1249
00:41:37,790 --> 00:41:39,660
-There's animal life here.

1250
00:41:39,660 --> 00:41:40,160
-Yeah.

1251
00:41:40,160 --> 00:41:41,830
White caribou.

1252
00:41:41,830 --> 00:41:42,650
-Cute.

1253
00:41:42,650 --> 00:41:44,270
-Listen, I saw a lake
 over in that direction

1254
00:41:44,270 --> 00:41:45,560
before we landed.
 Let's go over and take a look.

1255
00:41:45,560 --> 00:41:46,060
-OK.

1256
00:41:46,060 --> 00:41:48,760
-I think you mean you
 saw a clip of a lake.

1257
00:41:48,760 --> 00:41:50,310
-Everyone got a date?

1258
00:41:50,310 --> 00:41:52,200
Let's have that progressive
 dinner we were talking about,

1259
00:41:52,200 --> 00:41:54,310
huh?

1260
00:41:54,310 --> 00:41:57,030
Let's just throw all the
 research out the window.

1261
00:41:57,030 --> 00:41:58,060
-Look, birds.

1262
00:41:58,060 --> 00:41:59,180
-They look like vultures.

1263
00:41:59,180 --> 00:42:00,700
-Ah, death loving vultures.

1264
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:01,700
Hm, neat.

1265
00:42:06,200 --> 00:42:08,030
-Look at that three.

1266
00:42:08,030 --> 00:42:09,980
-Ha, wish I could do that.

1267
00:42:09,980 --> 00:42:11,960
-Isn't he cute?

1268
00:42:11,960 --> 00:42:12,780
-Bam, bam!

1269
00:42:18,240 --> 00:42:21,250
-Well, there it is, just
 like I saw on the scope.

1270
00:42:21,250 --> 00:42:24,520
As pretty a lake as anyone would
 want, and it's all private.

1271
00:42:24,520 --> 00:42:25,020
-Restricted.

1272
00:42:25,020 --> 00:42:26,510
-What about that island?

1273
00:42:26,510 --> 00:42:27,420
-Oh.

1274
00:42:27,420 --> 00:42:28,920
-That's a strange-looking place.

1275
00:42:28,920 --> 00:42:31,050
-Wow, it must be scary.

1276
00:42:31,050 --> 00:42:32,320
-No sun over there.

1277
00:42:32,320 --> 00:42:33,430
-No son of mine.

1278
00:42:33,430 --> 00:42:35,200
-I'd like to have a look
 at that when we have time.

1279
00:42:35,200 --> 00:42:36,780
-I don't see why, Nora.

1280
00:42:36,780 --> 00:42:39,410
All they want in Washington
 are tests and samples

1281
00:42:39,410 --> 00:42:40,180
from wherever we land.

1282
00:42:40,180 --> 00:42:41,150
-Well, if you need souvenirs.

1283
00:42:41,150 --> 00:42:43,080
-They didn't say anything
 about extensive exploration.

1284
00:42:43,080 --> 00:42:44,870
-You conservative doctors.

1285
00:42:44,870 --> 00:42:46,780
-Hey, we're
 neoconservative doctors.

1286
00:42:46,780 --> 00:42:47,670
-I don't care.

1287
00:42:47,670 --> 00:42:50,960
All I can see is that water,
 and that means a bath, Nora.

1288
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:53,730
And let's get some clean
 clothes and have one, huh?

1289
00:42:53,730 --> 00:42:55,230
[GIGGLING]

1290
00:42:55,230 --> 00:42:56,100
-Oh, for fun.

1291
00:42:56,100 --> 00:42:56,600
-Ah-ah!

1292
00:42:56,600 --> 00:42:57,300
Ladies first, please.

1293
00:42:57,300 --> 00:42:58,530
[GIGGLING]

1294
00:42:58,530 --> 00:42:59,390
-OK.

1295
00:42:59,390 --> 00:43:00,820
You've got to love them.

1296
00:43:00,820 --> 00:43:01,770
Let's go.

1297
00:43:06,050 --> 00:43:07,720
-Who brought the sandwiches?

1298
00:43:07,720 --> 00:43:08,220
-Sandwiches?

1299
00:43:11,610 --> 00:43:12,920
-Ooh.

1300
00:43:12,920 --> 00:43:15,280
Quite a package he's got there.

1301
00:43:15,280 --> 00:43:15,780
-Mm-hmm.

1302
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:22,130
-Much further?

1303
00:43:22,130 --> 00:43:26,390
-I want some samples from
 that rock straight up there.

1304
00:43:26,390 --> 00:43:28,390
